Guard ScalarEvolution cross-PHI recursion (SIGBUS/stack overflow in trustc)

Trust carries exactly one local change to LLVM. Building the compiler crashed
trustc with SIGBUS inside LLVM ScalarEvolution ("cycle encountered after 4
frames with period 15" through createAddRecFromPHI / createSimpleAffineAddRec /
createSCEV): createSimpleAffineAddRec evaluates the start/accum operands via
getSCEV BEFORE any placeholder is inserted into ValueExprMap, so a chain of
header PHIs whose operands reach back to the first PHI recurses unboundedly --
the general path's symbolic-name cycle breaker is never reached, because the
simple path runs first on every re-entry.

Fix: install the symbolic placeholder (getUnknown(PN)) into ValueExprMap
BEFORE the simple affine path, exactly the discipline the general path
already uses, and purge it with forgetMemoizedResults before memoizing the
precise AddRec. Re-entry then returns the placeholder instead of recursing,
and nothing keeps the over-approximation.

An earlier revision (1d1d11bed9f) instead guarded re-entry with a
PendingAddRecPHIs set returning nullptr. That stopped the recursion but left
SCEVUnknown memoized for PHIs reached during the cycle, which broke
IndVarSimplify's congruent-IV merging (measured 2026-08-24 on
tests/codegen-llvm/bounds-checking/gep-issue-133979.rs). Do not reintroduce
the bailout -- fix the ordering. The in-tree code comments carry the full
account at both sites.

Validated by full stage-2 builds on default stacks with zero SIGBUS; the
crash was previously reproducible, and RUST_MIN_STACK=128M only outran it,
which confirmed unbounded recursion rather than a deep-but-finite stack.

Applies to the LLVM 23.1.x line (the tree pinned by rustc 1.101.0-nightly,
llvm-project 915c669d232e). Upstream LLVM releases -- including distribution
packages such as Homebrew's llvm@23 -- carry the unguarded code, so an
external LLVM must be patched and rebuilt before it can host a Trust build.
See patches/README.md.

Origin: Trust commit 1d1d11bed9fa32dfa20e45592dd748b0fef64e4d, revised to the
placeholder-first form during the 1.99 development line; regenerated against
LLVM 23.1 during the Rust 1.101 migration (2026-10-16).

